<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Salihids</h1><p class="st-meta">400 CE – 502 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Banu Salih, the leading Arab allies of the Eastern Roman Empire in the 5th century, between the Tanukhids and the Ghassanids.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Salih were an Arab tribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qudaa">Quda'a</a> group who entered Roman territory in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-transjordan">Transjordan</a>. During the 5th century they displaced the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tanukhids">Tanukhids</a> as the principal foederati, guarding the desert frontier and supplying troops in return for annual payments.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Known mainly from later Arabic sources and scattered Greek references, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-nekor">Salihids</a> were Christian and their leaders appear to have founded monasteries; Arabic tradition remembers a king named Dawud who built one. They helped defend the frontier against nomad raids and the Lakhmid allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Details of their government and territory are uncertain.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>According to Arabic tradition, disputes over the tribute they collected from newly arrived Ghassanid tribes led to fighting in which the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ghassanid-kingdom">Ghassanids</a> prevailed. Around 502 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> concluded a treaty with the Ghassanids, who took the Salihids' place. The Salih survived as a tribe and fought on the Byzantine side against the early Muslim armies.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/salihids.jpg" alt="Salihids" width="320" height="200"><figcaption>Image: MWahaiibii · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salihids_Map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 CE – 502 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dawud</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>, <a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banu Salih, Salih</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=451&amp;focus=salihids" aria-label="Salihids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5643 898 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Salihids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M5922 1235h0M5922 1235h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5963 1154h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5963 1154h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Syria. <a href="/atlas?year=451&amp;focus=salihids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
